title: Marker Fields
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

Marker tiddlers carry a handful of fields. They cluster into four jobs: ''what triggers the match'', ''what HTML comes out'', ''how the body is consumed'', and ''how the marker shows up in the editor''. Most markers only need fields from the first two clusters; the others are opt-in for specific behaviour.

!! Identity and matching

How the engine recognises an occurrence of this marker in source text.

|! Field |! Required |! Notes |
|`kind` |yes |One of `glyph`, `glyph-level`, `word`, `inline-pair`, `linked-pair`, `list-item`. See [[Marker Kinds]]. |
|`open` |yes |Literal characters that trigger the marker. Always required as a stable identity key, even when `open-pattern` is set. |
|`close` |inline-pair, linked-pair |Literal characters that terminate the body. For linked-pair, this is the close of the first capture (e.g. `]` in `[text](url)`). |
|`link-open` |no |linked-pair only. Literal opening the second bracketed capture. Default `(`. |
|`link-close` |no |linked-pair only. Literal closing the second bracketed capture. Default `)`. |
|`open-pattern` |list-item |Regex source string. When set, overrides `open` for matching. Lets a single marker handle a bullet family (e.g. `\d+\.` for ordered lists). |
|`max-level` |glyph-level |Maximum repetitions of `open` that count as deeper levels. `»` `»»` `»»»` becomes `wltc-l1` `wltc-l2` `wltc-l3`. |
|`allow-symbol` |glyph, inline-pair |Default `yes`. Set `no` for body-only markers (where there's no symbol between open and content). Required for prose-safe `.` and emphasis-style inline pairs. |

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/default/DOT]]. A glyph marker that doesn't take a symbol.

```
title: vocab/default/DOT
kind: glyph
open: .
allow-symbol: no
```

Why each field is set:

* `kind: glyph` because the marker fires once per line, not in pairs and not repeating for levels.
* `open: .` is the single character users type at the start of a line.
* `allow-symbol: no` because `.` is everywhere in prose (`.NET`, `e.g.`). With it on, the engine would treat the first non-whitespace run after the dot as a symbol name and eat real content. Off, the marker only fires when `.` is immediately followed by whitespace, ruling out the prose collisions.

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/default/ANGLE]]. A multi-level glyph.

```
title: vocab/default/ANGLE
kind: glyph-level
open: »
max-level: 4
classes: .wltc-angle
```

`»text` renders with class `wltc-l1 wltc-angle`, `»»text` with `wltc-l2 wltc-angle`, up to four levels. The engine reads repeats of `open` from the source and emits the matching level class so CSS can indent each level.

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/markdown/ITEM-OL]]. An ordered-list bullet using a regex.

```
title: vocab/markdown/ITEM-OL
kind: list-item
open: 1.
open-pattern: \d+\.
container: ol
```

`open: 1.` exists so the engine has a stable identity for active-set tracking; the actual matching uses `open-pattern: \d+\.`, which accepts any number of digits followed by a dot. Without `open-pattern`, you'd need a separate marker for every starting number.

!! Output structure

What HTML comes out when the marker fires.

|! Field |! Required |! Notes |
|`element` |no |HTML element name (e.g. `p`, `h2`, `code`, `blockquote`). Defaults to `div` for block markers, `span` for inline-pair, `a` for linked-pair. Prefix with `$` (e.g. `$link`, `$image`, `$transclude`) to emit a TW widget node instead of a plain element. |
|`classes` |no |Dot-prefixed CSS class chain (e.g. `.wltc-fountain-transition`). Multiple classes via `.cls-a.cls-b`. |
|`attributes` |no |JSON object of extra HTML attributes, e.g. `{"role":"note"}`. |
|`link-attribute` |no |linked-pair only. Name of the attribute that receives the LINK capture text. Default `href` (so the second `(...)` of a markdown link becomes `<a href="...">`). Set `to` for `$link` widget (tiddler title), `source` for `$image`. |
|`attr-<name>-from` |no |linked-pair only. Wire the rendered element's `<name>` attribute to a field on the source tiddler — as a ''fallback'' for when the user didn't supply the attribute via body / tooltip. At parse time the engine checks whether the LINK capture resolves to a tiddler that has the given field; if so AND `attrs[<name>]` is unset, the attribute is emitted as an indirect reference (`<linkText>!!<field>`) that stays reactive at render time. ''Special accessibility rule for `alt`:'' even when the user supplied an alt, if it's 1–2 words AND the source field is 3+ words, the source wins (richer is better for screen readers). Multiple `attr-<name>-from` fields can coexist (one per attribute). |
|`auto-external` |no |linked-pair only. `yes` enables TW prettylink-style routing: when the LINK capture matches an external URL pattern (`http://`, `https://`, `mailto:`, `ftp://`, ...) the configured `element` is swapped for a plain `<a href target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">`. Lets a single marker handle both internal tiddler titles AND external URLs cleanly. |
|`link-hash-prefix` |no |linked-pair only. Two values:<br>• `yes` — a leading `#` on the captured LINK is recognised as "force internal": the `#` is stripped, the rest is percent-decoded for the tiddler-title lookup, and `auto-external` is bypassed. Targets without `#` still fall through to the configured element (flexible / backward-compatible mode).<br>• `required` — same `#` recognition, AND targets without `#` are routed through `<a href target="_blank">` instead of falling through. Matches the TW markdown plugin's strict semantics ("everything is a URL unless explicitly marked internal"). |
|`link-tooltip-attribute` |no |linked-pair only. When set, an optional trailing `"..."` on the captured LINK (separated by whitespace) is peeled off and emitted on this attribute (`tooltip` for `<$link>` / `<$image>`, `title` for plain `<a>`). Matches the TW markdown plugin's `[text](target "tooltip")` syntax. |
|`link-angle-brackets` |no |linked-pair only. `yes` accepts `<...>` wrapping around the captured LINK so titles containing whitespace, `<`, `>`, or other awkward characters can be written. Inside the wrap, `\<` and `\>` unescape to literal angle brackets. Matches the TW markdown plugin's `[text](<#Title with spaces>)` syntax. |
|`link-resolve` |no |linked-pair only. `ref` turns the captured LINK text into a LABEL — looked up at parse time in `parser.cmRegistry.linkRefs` (populated by ref-def block rules like `markdown-ref-def`). On hit, the resolved target replaces the label and the def's optional `"title"` fills in for the marker's tooltip attribute. On miss, the marker emits its raw match text as literal characters (so an unresolved `[x][y]` shows as written). Defs must appear BEFORE their usages in the source (single-pass; no pre-scan). See [[vocab/markdown/LINK-REF]]. |
|`container` |list-item |The wrapping element for consecutive same-level items (`ul`, `ol`, `dl`, `blockquote`). |
|`item-element` |list-item |Element for each item inside the container (default `li`; use `dt`/`dd` for definition lists). |
|`url-pattern` |no |autolink only. Regex string the body must match for the marker to fire in URL mode. Used as-is for the emitted `link-attribute` (no prefix). When neither this nor `email-pattern` matches the body, the marker steps aside and the raw `<text>` renders as literal characters. CommonMark default: `^[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9+.-]{0,31}:[^\s<>]+$`. |
|`email-pattern` |no |autolink only. Regex string the body must match for the marker to fire in email mode. The body becomes the visible text; the `email-prefix` is prepended for the `link-attribute` value (so the displayed `user@host` gets a `mailto:user@host` href). CommonMark default: the long RFC 5322 pattern. A DSL can repurpose this slot for any second pattern that needs an href prefix (`tel:` for phone numbers, `obsidian:` for vault refs, etc.). |
|`email-prefix` |no |autolink only. String prepended to the `link-attribute` value when `email-pattern` matches. Default `mailto:`. |
|`wrapper-element` |no |fenced only. Optional outer element wrapping the inner `element` (e.g. `pre` so a code-block marker emits `<pre><code class="language-X">…</code></pre>`). When set, the marker's identity classes (the `.classes` chain plus the universal `wltc`) land on the WRAPPER — so a CSS rule like `.wltc { margin: 1em 0 }` adds breathing room outside the block, not inside it. When unset, only `element` is emitted and it carries the identity classes itself. |
|`info-attribute` |no |fenced only. Name of the attribute that receives the captured info-string first word (or all of it, when `info-words: all`). Placement depends on the attribute name:<br>• `class` — info value goes on the INNER element alone (CommonMark `<code class="language-X">` convention; syntax highlighters look there).<br>• anything else — info value goes on the OUTER wrapper element alongside the marker identity classes, so a CSS rule like `.wltc-md-codeblock[data-title]::before { content: attr(data-title); }` can match on a single element. (For markers without a `wrapper-element`, "outer" and "inner" are the same single element — placement is unambiguous.) When unset, the first-word info value is dropped — but `info-attrs: yes` can still consume the rest of the info string. |
|`info-prefix` |no |fenced only. String prepended to the info value before emission. CommonMark code blocks use `language-` so `` ` ` `js `` produces `class="… language-js"`. Has no effect when `info-attribute` is unset. |
|`info-words` |no |fenced only. Default `1` — only the first whitespace-delimited token of the info string goes into the info-attribute (CommonMark convention). Set `all` to emit the whole info string verbatim. With `info-attrs: yes`, the AFTER-first-word remainder is parsed separately (see the next row). |
|`info-attrs` |no |fenced only. `yes` parses the info string remainder (after the first word, when `info-attribute` is set) via TW's `parseMacroParameterAsAttribute` — same syntax as `<<macro arg=val arg:val>>` parameters. Each parsed attribute name is prepended with `data-` and emitted as an additional attribute on the same element that carries the non-class info-attribute (outer wrapper if present, else the single element). All canonical TW value syntaxes work: `name="quoted"`, `name=unquoted`, `name:value`, `name={{!!field}}`, `name={{{filter}}}`, `name=<<macro>>`, `name=` `` `subst` ``. The `data-` prefix is hardcoded and non-overridable — it's the security boundary, so a user-supplied `onclick="alert(1)"` lands as the inert `data-onclick="alert(1)"`. |

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/fountain/INT]]. A Fountain scene heading.

```
title: vocab/fountain/INT
kind: word
open: INT.
element: p
classes: .wltc-fountain-scene-heading
emit-open: yes
```

* `element: p` makes the output an HTML paragraph the screenplay CSS can style.
* `classes: .wltc-fountain-scene-heading` is the CSS hook for the screenplay layout (left-aligned, uppercase, etc.).
* `emit-open: yes` keeps `INT.` in the rendered output. Without it the keyword would be consumed and only `KITCHEN MORNING` would render. Authors expect `INT. KITCHEN MORNING` to read back as written.

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/markdown/ITEM-DASH]]. A markdown list-item.

```
title: vocab/markdown/ITEM-DASH
kind: list-item
open: -
container: ul
item-element: li
classes: .wltc-md-list-item
```

`-` triggers a list item. The engine builds `<ul><li>...</li></ul>` around consecutive matches. Deeper-indented matches nest under the previous item; same-indent siblings extend the current `<ul>`. The `container` and `item-element` fields tell the engine which wrapper tags to emit.

!! Parsing behaviour

How the engine consumes content between the opening marker and the next block break.

|! Field |! Required |! Notes |
|`mode` |no |`block` (default for non-inline-pair) parses body as multi-block content; `inline` parses as a single inline run. Use `inline` for headings, scene-heading-style markers, anything where the body is one line. |
|`paragraph-marker` |no |`yes` opts into blank-line terminator (instead of the default single-newline). Required when wrapping multi-paragraph content. |
|`end-string` |no |Custom terminator string. Overrides the kind-driven default. Use for explicit close keywords (e.g. Fountain's `FADE OUT.`). |
|`emit-open` |no |`yes` prepends the open literal to the rendered body so the keyword IS the content (Fountain transitions render `CUT TO:` literally, not as an empty `<p>`). |
|`body-raw` |inline-pair |`yes` captures the body as a single literal text node, no recursive parsing. Required for code-span markers where inside-content must stay literal. |
|`open-variable` |inline-pair |`yes` turns the marker into a variable-length delimiter run: the `open` field is treated as a SINGLE character that can repeat 1..N times at parse time, and the close must match the SAME N. Both ends are flanked by non-open characters (or string boundaries) so the run is unambiguously maximal. Body is captured verbatim (implies `body-raw`). CommonMark single-leading/trailing-space stripping is applied when the body has both flanks AND non-whitespace content. Canonical use: CommonMark inline code spans (single, double and tripple backtics `` ` ``) — see [[vocab/markdown/CODE]]. |
|`body-attribute` |no |linked-pair only. When set, the body between `open` and `close` is captured verbatim and emitted as a string attribute of this name on the rendered element (no inline parsing, no children). Used by markdown's IMAGE marker with `body-attribute: alt` so `![alt](src "tooltip")` puts the body into `alt` and the trailing `"…"` into `tooltip` — mirroring CommonMark's `![alt](src "title")` mapping. When the user-supplied body is empty, the attribute is skipped so an `attr-X-from` default can take over. Without `body-attribute`, the body parses as an inline run and becomes the rendered element's children (link-text case). |
|`indent-unit` |list-item |Spaces of leading indent that count as one nesting level (default `2`). Tabs are expanded to this many spaces before the level is computed. |
|`src-name` |widget elements |Widget-element markers only (`element` starts with `$`). Name of the attribute that receives the marker body. Defaults to `src`. Common values: `code` for `$macrocall` of a procedure that takes a code body, `filter` for `$list`, `body` for any custom procedure body parameter. |

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/markdown/HEADING-2]]. A markdown heading.

```
title: vocab/markdown/HEADING-2
kind: word
open: ##
mode: inline
element: h2
```

`mode: inline` is the critical line. Without it, `## My heading` renders as `<h2><p>My heading</p></h2>` because the engine defaults block-kind bodies to a paragraph parse. With `mode: inline`, the body parses as a single inline run, giving `<h2>My heading</h2>`. Headings are always single-line content, never multi-paragraph.

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/markdown/CODE]]. An inline code span with literal body and variable-length delimiter run.

```
title: vocab/markdown/CODE
kind: inline-pair
open: `
close: `
allow-symbol: no
element: code
body-raw: yes
open-variable: yes
```

* `body-raw: yes` keeps the body verbatim — without it, `<h1>foo</h1>` would emit an actual `<h1>` element instead of literal text.
* `open-variable: yes` lets the delimiter run grow: a double-backtick span containing a literal single backtick
```
`` ` ``
```

* The closing run must have the SAME number of backticks as the opening run and must not be flanked by another backtick. That's how the parser knows where the span ends without needing the author to escape inner backticks.

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/default/ANGLE]]. A paragraph-style marker.

```
title: vocab/default/ANGLE
kind: glyph-level
open: »
element: p
paragraph-marker: yes
```

`paragraph-marker: yes` switches the terminator from single newline to blank line, so a `»` paragraph can wrap multiple lines without each line becoming its own `<p>`. Required whenever the marker represents prose that flows across line breaks.

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/demo/HELLO]]. A marker that calls a procedure with a renamed body parameter.

```
title: vocab/demo/HELLO
kind: word
open: HELLO
mode: block
end-string: ---
element: $transclude
src-name: body
attributes: {"$variable": ".greet", "$mode": "block", "name": "World", "mood": "happy"}
```

`src-name: body` means the marker body lands in the `body` parameter of the called procedure (rather than the default `src`). See [[Example - Marker as procedure call]] for the full pattern.

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/markdown/LINK]]. A markdown link routed through TW's link widget, with prettylink-style internal/external split and TW-markdown-plugin syntax compatibility.

```
title: vocab/markdown/LINK
kind: linked-pair
open: [
close: ]
link-open: (
link-close: )
element: $link
link-attribute: to
auto-external: yes
link-hash-prefix: required
link-angle-brackets: yes
link-tooltip-attribute: tooltip
classes: .wltc-md-link
```

* `kind: linked-pair` picks the back-to-back-brackets matching mode.
* `open: [` / `close: ]` / `link-open: (` / `link-close: )` are the four bracket literals — the engine assembles the regex `\[BODY\]\(LINK\)` from these.
* `element: $link` makes the engine emit a `<$link>` widget instead of a plain element. TW's link widget treats `to` as a tiddler title (opens in the story river).
* `link-attribute: to` wires the LINK capture into the widget's `to` parameter (rather than the default `href`).
* `auto-external: yes` swaps `<$link>` for `<a href target=_blank>` whenever the LINK capture looks like an external URL. Same prettylink routing TW core's `[[Caption|target]]` does.
* `link-hash-prefix: required`, `link-angle-brackets: yes`, `link-tooltip-attribute: tooltip` together implement the TW markdown plugin's full strict syntax: `[x](#Title)`, `[x](<#Title with spaces>)`, `[x](#Title "tooltip")`, and the most general `[x](<#Title with \<escapes\>> "tooltip")`. The `required` value means a bare `[x](Title)` (no `#`, not URL-shaped) gets routed as external `<a href="Title">` — same behaviour as the TW md plugin. Use `link-hash-prefix: yes` instead to keep the legacy "bare title → internal" fall-through.

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/markdown/IMAGE]]. A markdown image routed through TW's image widget, with source-tiddler-field wiring and TW-markdown-plugin syntax compatibility.

```
title: vocab/markdown/IMAGE
kind: linked-pair
open: ![
close: ]
link-open: (
link-close: )
element: $image
link-attribute: source
body-attribute: alt
attr-alt-from: alt-text
attr-tooltip-from: alt-text
link-hash-prefix: yes
link-angle-brackets: yes
link-tooltip-attribute: tooltip
classes: .wltc-md-image
```

* `open: ![` shadows the LINK marker because `![` is longer — the combined inline regex sorts longest-open-first, so IMAGE matches before LINK at any position they could both fire.
* `element: $image` + `link-attribute: source` makes the LINK capture land in the image widget's `source` parameter (which handles BOTH tiddler titles AND external URLs natively).
* `body-attribute: alt` means the bracketed body text becomes the `alt` attribute. Mirrors CommonMark: `![alt](src "title")` — body is alt, `"title"` is tooltip.
* `link-tooltip-attribute: tooltip` peels off the trailing `"..."` parameter and emits it on `tooltip`.
* `attr-alt-from: alt-text` / `attr-tooltip-from: alt-text` provide ''fallbacks'' from the source tiddler's `alt-text` field for both attributes. User-supplied values win; the source fills in when absent. ''Plus the smart-alt rule:'' a 1–2 word user-alt yields to a 3+ word source `alt-text` (richer is better for screen readers).
* `link-hash-prefix` / `link-angle-brackets` / `link-tooltip-attribute` are the same TW-md-plugin compat triple as on LINK, so `![alt](#Image.jpg "tooltip")` and `![alt](<#Image with spaces>)` work in parity.

''Worked example:'' [[vocab/markdown/CODE-BLOCK]]. A fenced code block emitting CommonMark's `<pre><code class="language-X">...</code></pre>` shape, with MkDocs / Hugo style attribute metadata.

```
title: vocab/markdown/CODE-BLOCK
kind: fenced
open: ```
element: code
wrapper-element: pre
info-attribute: class
info-prefix: language-
info-words: 1
info-attrs: yes
classes: .wltc-md-codeblock
```

* `kind: fenced` picks the block-level fenced matching mode: a line-leading run of `>= open.length` fence chars opens; the same character with run `>= N` on its own line closes.
* `open:` `` ` ` ` `` ''tripple backtick'' (no spaces) pins the fence character (backtick) and the minimum run length (3). Authors can use longer runs (4, 5, 6 ...) so the body can contain shorter backtick runs without terminating early.
* `element: code` + `wrapper-element: pre` together produce the `<pre><code>...</code></pre>` shape conventional for code blocks. The OUTER `<pre>` carries the marker identity classes (`.wltc-md-codeblock wltc`) plus any `data-*` from `info-attrs`; the INNER `<code>` carries only the language class.
* `info-attribute: class` + `info-prefix: language-` + `info-words: 1` together implement CommonMark's `language-X` class convention: only the first whitespace-delimited token of the info string is used, it gets `language-` prepended, and the result lands on the inner `<code>` as `class="language-X"` — exactly where Prism / highlight.js / Shiki look for it.
* `info-attrs: yes` parses the AFTER-first-word remainder of the info string via TW's macro-parameter attribute parser and emits each `name=value` (or `name:value`) as `data-{name}` on the outer `<pre>`. `` ` ` `python title="src/auth.py" highlight=2,5 `` becomes `<pre data-title="src/auth.py" data-highlight="2,5">`. The hardcoded `data-` prefix is the security boundary — see the `info-attrs` field row for the threat model.

!! Editor integration and back-compat

Optional fields that affect the editor toolbar and legacy pragma resolution.

|! Field |! Required |! Notes |
|`marker-icon` |no |Title of the SVG icon tiddler shown in the toolbar dropdown. Conventionally under `$:/plugins/wikilabs/custom-markup/icons/glyphs/...` or `.../inline/...`. |
|`order` |no |Sort hint for editor-toolbar dropdowns. ''Higher'' number sorts earlier (more prominent); unset markers fall back to caption order. vocab/default uses `ANGLE=4`, `SINGLE=3`, `DOT=2`, `DEGREE=1`. |
|`legacy-kind` |no |Friendly name (`tick`, `degree`, ...) that the v0.x `\custom <kind>=<symbol>` pragma can still resolve to this marker. |
|`allow-classes` |no |Word-kind only. `yes` lets users append a class chain after the keyword: `CUT TO:.urgent`. Default off. |
|`caption` |yes |Display name in marker-summary headers and editor dropdowns. |
|`description` |no |One-sentence summary surfaced in the marker-summary view. |

!! Security

|! Field |! Required |! Notes |
|`allow-unsafe` |no |Default secure-by-default: iframe gets `sandbox=""` injected, `object`/`embed`/`frame`/`frameset` are denied, `javascript:` URL values are stripped. Set `yes` to opt out. See [[Custom Markup Security]] for the threat model and the full sanitiser rules. |

!! Debugging

Optional fields that emit a render-time codeblock alongside (or instead of) the normal output. Useful for diagnosing why a marker isn't rendering as expected. See [[Debugging markers]] for the per-tiddler `\debugmarker` override and worked examples.

|! Field |! Required |! Notes |
|`debug` |no |One of `pragma` (default if truthy), `pragmaOnly`, `text`, `textOnly`, `both`, `no`. `*Only` modes suppress the normal render. |
|`debug-string` |no |Custom codeblock content for `pragma` / `pragmaOnly` / `both` modes. When empty, auto-generated from the marker's metadata. |